tryLock
- obj.lock(), with this thread will block until the lock becomes available, but with obj.tryLock(), the thread will not block. it returns true or false depending on whether the lock is held 
by another thread and simply continue execution.
- We also have tryLock with timeout. 


Read-Write locks
- We have bunch of threads only reading values of shared vars, less threads only writing data to shared vars. Allow to give exclusive access to writer threads and
allow reader threads to read the var.
- This is when we can use ReadWriteLock rw = new ReentrantReadWriteLock()
- readLock = rw.readLock(), writeLock = rw.wtireLock()
- readLock.lock(), writeLock.lock()
- idea is read lock should be used for read threads, and write lock for write threads.
- if read threads are trying to acqure the lock, all of them will be able to acquire the lock. But when writer thread is trying to acquire the data, 
read threads will wait in the lock operation. Writers have priority but they will get the lock only when all readers have given up the lock.
- We define 2 CS - one for write and one for read


This construction here is a spin lock or busy waiting : 
while (true)
{
boolean acquired = readLock.tryLock();
if (acquired) break;
else Sopln("Waiting for read lock");
}

- run loop while continuously trying to get lock, this is different from lockObj.lock() as lock() puts current thread in waiting state 
and spin lock works keeps current thread on cpu i.e. in runnable state until lock is able to aquired.
- not recommended to use it for long wait time operation


Condition Variables for reentrant locks
- variables that help us communicate between threads similar to monitors.
- Condition condition = lock.newCondition() 
- condition.await(), condition.signal() to be called only inside critical section.
- await will put thread to sleep and release the lock, similar to wait()
- signal() thread in await will be release but only happens when thread 2 who is in critical section releases the lock, similar to notify
- really similar to wait-notify
- why was this needed then? we can create more conditions on same lock and more complex synchronization scenarios.
- whereas on object, there's only one monitor on an object.


Semaphores
- Semaphore semaphore = new Semaphore(permits)
- semaphore.aquire(), semaphore.release()
- semaphore has limit on num of aquire calls we can make depending on permits, so number of threads that can call aquire depends on permits
- release will increase the permit
- with locks, only 1 thread can execute CS. With semaphore, as many as permits num of threads can enter CS
- with semaphore, aquire and release can be called on different threads unlike locks
- we can also have negative permits, means we need to first call release methods until we can acquire a permit.
- we can also have aquire(permit), reelase(permit) meaning one thread will aquire permit num of permits at a time








